    <!-- 3. Variables and data types -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Variables and data types" data-notes="Apex is strongly typed: every variable is declared with a type up front. Walk through the primitive types, then mention sObject and collections come later in the session.">
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 1 · Variables, types, operators, control flow</span>
        <h2>Variables: strongly typed, always</h2>
        <p class="body">Every Apex variable is declared with a type, then a name, and optionally an initial value: <code>Integer age = 30;</code>. Apex will not let you put the wrong kind of value into a variable.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Holds</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Integer</td><td>Whole numbers, 32-bit</td><td><code>Integer qty = 5;</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Long</td><td>Whole numbers, 64-bit, for very large values</td><td><code>Long bigNum = 3000000000L;</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Decimal</td><td>Fixed-point numbers, safe for money</td><td><code>Decimal price = 19.99;</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Double</td><td>Floating-point numbers</td><td><code>Double pi = 3.14159;</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>String</td><td>Text</td><td><code>String name = 'Rahul';</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Boolean</td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>Boolean isActive = true;</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Date</td><td>Calendar date, no time</td><td><code>Date d = Date.today();</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Datetime</td><td>Date and time together</td><td><code>Datetime dt = Datetime.now();</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Id</td><td>A Salesforce record Id</td><td><code>Id accId = acc.Id;</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>sObject</td><td>Any Salesforce record (Account, custom object...)</td><td><code>Account acc = new Account();</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Tip: use <code>Decimal</code> for money, never <code>Double</code> — floating point rounding can silently produce the wrong cents.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. Operators -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Operators" data-notes="Nothing exotic here, but the comparison and logical operators matter a lot once we get to control flow and later triggers. Point out == compares value, not reference, for primitives.">
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 1 · Variables, types, operators, control flow</span>
        <h2>Operators: doing things with values</h2>
        <div class="tabgroup">
          <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
            <button class="tab on" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="ar">Arithmetic</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="cp">Comparison</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="lg">Logical</button>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="ar"><dl>
            <dt><code>+ - * /</code></dt><dd>Add, subtract, multiply, divide.</dd>
            <dt><code>%</code></dt><dd>Modulo — the remainder after division, e.g. <code>10 % 3</code> is <code>1</code>.</dd>
            <dt><code>++ --</code></dt><dd>Increment and decrement a number by one.</dd>
          </dl></div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="cp" hidden><dl>
            <dt><code>== !=</code></dt><dd>Equal to, not equal to. For primitives this compares the value.</dd>
            <dt><code>&gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;=</code></dt><dd>Greater than, less than, and their "or equal to" versions.</dd>
          </dl></div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="lg" hidden><dl>
            <dt><code>&amp;&amp;</code></dt><dd>AND — both sides must be true.</dd>
            <dt><code>||</code></dt><dd>OR — at least one side must be true.</dd>
            <dt><code>!</code></dt><dd>NOT — flips a Boolean's value.</dd>
          </dl></div>
        </div>
