    <!-- 5. Control flow -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Control flow" data-notes="These are the same shapes as most languages. The one Salesforce-specific habit to plant early: never put a SOQL query or DML inside these loops when working with a collection of records — that comes back in the bulk-safe slide later.">
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 1 · Variables, types, operators, control flow</span>
        <h2>Control flow: making decisions and repeating</h2>
        <div class="tabgroup">
          <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
            <button class="tab on" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="if">if / else</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="for">for loop</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="wh">while / do-while</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="sw">switch</button>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="if">
            <p class="body">Runs a block only if a condition is true, with an optional fallback.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>if (score &gt;= 90) {
    grade = 'A';
} else if (score &gt;= 75) {
    grade = 'B';
} else {
    grade = 'C';
}</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="for" hidden>
            <p class="body">The workhorse for iterating a fixed number of times, or over a collection.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>for (Integer i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    System.debug(i);
}

for (Account acc : accountList) {
    System.debug(acc.Name);
}</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="wh" hidden>
            <p class="body"><code>while</code> checks the condition first; <code>do-while</code> runs the body at least once before checking.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>Integer n = 0;
while (n &lt; 3) {
    System.debug(n);
    n++;
}

Integer m = 0;
do {
    System.debug(m);
    m++;
} while (m &lt; 3);</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="sw" hidden>
            <p class="body">A clean alternative to a long <code>if / else if</code> chain when checking one value against several options.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>switch on status {
    when 'New' {
        System.debug('Just created');
    }
    when 'Closed', 'Cancelled' {
        System.debug('No longer active');
    }
    when else {
        System.debug('In progress');
    }
}</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. Collections: List -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Collections: List" data-notes="A List is ordered and allows duplicates, accessed by index starting at zero. Show adding, getting by index, and looping. Mention List<Account> is the type you'll see constantly from SOQL query results.">
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 2 · Collections</span>
        <h2>List: an ordered collection</h2>
        <p class="body">A <strong>List</strong> keeps items in the order you add them, allows duplicates, and is accessed by a numeric index starting at <code>0</code>. Every SOQL query returns a <code>List</code>.</p>
        <div class="copyable">
          <pre class="code"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names = new List&lt;String&gt;();
names.add('Rahul');
names.add('Priya');
names.add('Rahul');          // duplicates are fine

System.debug(names.size());   // 3
System.debug(names[0]);       // Rahul
names.remove(1);              // removes 'Priya'

for (String n : names) {
    System.debug(n);
}</code></pre>
          <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Best for: preserving order, allowing duplicates, and holding SOQL query results such as <code>List&lt;Account&gt;</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. Collections: Set -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Collections: Set" data-notes="A Set has no order and no duplicates — perfect for collecting unique Ids before a single bulk query. This is the pattern from Session 2's bulk-safe example.">
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 2 · Collections</span>
        <h2>Set: unique values, no order</h2>
        <p class="body">A <strong>Set</strong> automatically removes duplicates and has no guaranteed order. It's the natural choice when you only care whether something exists, not how many times.</p>
        <div class="copyable">
          <pre class="code"><code>Set&lt;Id&gt; accountIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();
accountIds.add(acc1.Id);
accountIds.add(acc2.Id);
accountIds.add(acc1.Id);      // ignored, already present

System.debug(accountIds.size());       // 2
System.debug(accountIds.contains(acc1.Id)); // true</code></pre>
          <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Best for: collecting unique Ids from a loop before running one bulk SOQL query with <code>WHERE Id IN :accountIds</code> — the single most common Set use case in Apex.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8. Collections: Map -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Collections: Map" data-notes="A Map pairs a unique key to a value — think of it as a lookup table in memory. This is exactly what you get back when you wrap a query result in new Map<Id, Account>(...)." >
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 2 · Collections</span>
        <h2>Map: key-value pairs</h2>
        <p class="body">A <strong>Map</strong> links a unique key to a value, so you can look something up instantly instead of looping to find it.</p>
        <div class="copyable">
          <pre class="code"><code>Map&lt;Id, Account&gt; accByid = new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;(
    [SELECT Id, Name FROM Account LIMIT 200]
);

Account acc = accByid.get(someId);
Boolean exists = accByid.containsKey(someId);

for (Id key : accByid.keySet()) {
    System.debug(accByid.get(key).Name);
}</code></pre>
          <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Best for: fast lookups by Id, and for turning a query result into something you can access in O(1) instead of looping every time.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 9. Which collection? decision guide -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Which collection do I need?" data-notes="Click each scenario and let the audience guess first. The pattern to reinforce: List for order/duplicates, Set for uniqueness, Map for lookups by key.">
