    <!-- 10. Classes and objects -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Classes and objects" data-notes="A class is a blueprint; an object (instance) is what you get when you use 'new'. Point out that every Apex file you create in VS Code is a class.">
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 3 · Classes, methods, constructors, access modifiers</span>
        <h2>Classes: blueprints for objects</h2>
        <p class="body">A <strong>class</strong> defines the shape of something — its data (fields/properties) and behavior (methods). An <strong>object</strong> is a specific instance created from that blueprint with <code>new</code>.</p>
        <div class="copyable">
          <pre class="code"><code>public class SupportTicket {
    public String subject;
    public String priority;

    public String describe() {
        return subject + ' (' + priority + ')';
    }
}

// Creating an instance
SupportTicket t = new SupportTicket();
t.subject = 'Login issue';
t.priority = 'High';
System.debug(t.describe());</code></pre>
          <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 11. Methods -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Methods" data-notes="A method's signature is return type, name, and parameters. Static methods belong to the class itself and are called without creating an instance — this trips up beginners the most." >
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 3 · Classes, methods, constructors, access modifiers</span>
        <h2>Methods: a class's behavior</h2>
        <div class="tabgroup">
          <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
            <button class="tab on" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="inst">Instance method</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="stat">Static method</button>
            <button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" data-tab="ret">Return types</button>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="inst">
            <p class="body">Belongs to a specific object. You must create an instance first, then call it on that instance.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>SupportTicket t = new SupportTicket();
t.subject = 'Login issue';
System.debug(t.describe());  // called on t</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="stat" hidden>
            <p class="body">Belongs to the class itself, not to any one instance. Call it directly on the class name — no <code>new</code> needed.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>public class MathHelper {
    public static Integer square(Integer n) {
        return n * n;
    }
}

Integer result = MathHelper.square(5); // 25, no 'new' needed</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel" data-panel="ret" hidden>
            <p class="body">A method either returns a value of a declared type, or returns nothing with <code>void</code>.</p>
            <div class="copyable">
              <pre class="code"><code>public Integer add(Integer a, Integer b) {
    return a + b;         // must return an Integer
}

public void logMessage(String msg) {
    System.debug(msg);    // returns nothing
}</code></pre>
              <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 12. Constructors -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Constructors" data-notes="A constructor has the same name as the class and no return type. If you write no constructor at all, Apex gives you a free no-argument one — but as soon as you add any constructor of your own, that free one disappears." >
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 3 · Classes, methods, constructors, access modifiers</span>
        <h2>Constructors: setting up a new object</h2>
        <p class="body">A <strong>constructor</strong> runs automatically when you create an object with <code>new</code>. It has the same name as the class and no return type — its job is to set the object up correctly from the start.</p>
        <div class="copyable">
          <pre class="code"><code>public class SupportTicket {
    public String subject;
    public String priority;

    // Custom constructor
    public SupportTicket(String subject, String priority) {
        this.subject = subject;
        this.priority = priority;
    }
}

// No more no-argument new SupportTicket() — you must pass both values
SupportTicket t = new SupportTicket('Login issue', 'High');</code></pre>
          <button class="btn copyb" type="button">Copy code</button>
        </div>
        <p class="note">You can define more than one constructor with different parameters — this is called <strong>constructor overloading</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 13. Access modifiers -->
    <section class="slide" data-title="Access modifiers" data-notes="private is the default if you write nothing. global is rare and mostly for managed packages — most beginner code should never need it. with sharing/without sharing control record-level security, not code visibility, and are set on the class itself." >
      <div class="slide-inner">
        <span class="chip">Topic 3 · Classes, methods, constructors, access modifiers</span>
        <h2>Access modifiers: who can see what</h2>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Modifier</th><th>Visible from</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>private</td><td>The same class only. This is the default if you write nothing.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>public</td><td>Anywhere in the same application/namespace.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>protected</td><td>The same class and any classes that extend it.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>global</td><td>Anywhere, including other namespaces and managed packages. Rarely needed as a beginner.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Separately, <code>with sharing</code> and <code>without sharing</code> on a class control whether the current user's record-level sharing rules are enforced — that's about data access, not code visibility, and we'll cover it properly in the Security session.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

